obs the day's real lesson was procedural rather than technical: a Write tool call will cheerfully overwrite a file that already exists and is already correct, and the harness's own "changed on disk" reminder is the signal to stop and diff rather than to proceed on the assumption that absence-from-the-roster means absence-from-disk
obs two round-trips through the fleet-claims.kyri conflict pattern confirm it is mechanical and low-risk: the board moves fast enough that a rebase almost always meets a newer claim, and the resolution is always "keep the live one, drop the one this commit is closing"
rota lap 7551, row 1 -- Air, the row that feels. Re-read foundations/20260826-021732_air-the-row-that-feels.md: air's question is where does each thing end, tested by touch rather than by claim. This lap pressed on two real edges and both held under the hand -- the ITINERARY pin's own byte bound (it refused at 494 over, and shedding the predecessor account closed the gap exactly) and the mechanism-sentence commit wall (it refused twice for real, not performatively, until three distinct words actually stood in the body). Both boundaries did their job: caught a hand that had drifted, in time to fix it before it shipped.
